==Old English==

===Etymology 1===
From Germanic ''*fēra-''. Cognate with Old Saxon ''[[var|vār]]'' ‘ambush’ (Dutch ''[[gevaar]]'' ‘danger’), Old High German ''[[fara|fāra]]'' ‘ambush, danger, deceit’ (German ''[[Gefahr]]'' ‘danger’).

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/fæːr/|lang=ang}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|m|head=fǣr}}

# sudden [[danger]], [[calamity]]; [[ambush]]

=====Derived terms=====
* [[færgripe|fǣrgripe]]
* [[færgryre|fǣrgryre]]

=====Descendants=====
* English: [[fear]]

===Etymology 2===
From the same Germanic source as {{term|faran|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old High German ''[[fuora]]'' (German {{term|Fuhre|lang=de}}).

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/fær/|lang=ang}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|n}}

# [[journey]]; [[coming]], [[going]]

=====Descendants=====
* English: [[fare]]

